<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aswan High Dam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1960">1960</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aswan High Dam is a rock-fill dam across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> at Aswan in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, built between 1960 and 1970 with Soviet finance and engineering after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> withdrew their offer of support in 1956. It ended the annual Nile flood, provided water for irrigation and much of Egypt's electricity, and created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a>, one of the largest artificial lakes in the world. Its reservoir displaced some 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a> and required the rescue of ancient monuments such as the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam.jpg" alt="Aswan High Dam" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: شرف الدين · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aswan_High_Dam-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>'s annual flood enriched the soil of its valley and delta, which made the valley ideal for farming since ancient times, but the flood varied: high-water years could destroy a whole crop, and low-water years could bring drought and famine. The earliest recorded attempt to build a dam near Aswan came in the 11th century, when the Fatimid Caliph Al-Hakim bi-Amr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> summoned the engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-haytham">Ibn al-Haytham</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to regulate the flooding. His field work convinced him the scheme was impractical, and, fearing the Caliph's anger, he feigned madness.</p><p>The British began the Aswan Low Dam in 1898, and it opened on 10 December 1902, but its small capacity let water pass largely unimpeded. In 1952 the Greek-Egyptian engineer Adrian Daninos began to plan a new dam. After the monarchy was overthrown, the Free Officers, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a>, were convinced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>'s waters had to be stored in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for political reasons, and accepted Daninos's plan within two months. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the USSR were both at first interested in helping, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> rivalry complicated matters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The High Dam was built between 1960 and 1970, 7 km upstream of the Low Dam, with help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. It was designed by Nikolai Aleksandrovich Malyshev of the Moscow-based Hydroproject Institute, with features new in the 1960s, including a very deep grout curtain below its base. It is 3,830 metres long and 111 metres tall, and when finished it was the tallest earthen dam in the world. Its reservoir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a>, is 500 km long and covers 5,250 square kilometres. The dam releases on average 55 cubic kilometres of water a year, of which some 46 are diverted into irrigation canals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The dam brought protection from floods and droughts, more agricultural production and employment, electricity and better navigation, which also benefits tourism. It flooded a large area, relocating over 100,000 people, and submerged many archaeological sites while others were moved. It is blamed for coastline erosion, soil salinity and health problems. Its costs and benefits remain controversial. One observer calls its impacts overwhelmingly positive, while another recommended tearing it down.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aswan High Dam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
